Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Updated Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report  MS 08  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  CASE  MS 08  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  Date Taken  REMOVED_DATE Source Care Unit  Same Day Surgery  WACC 3 Path Subspecialty Service  Breast 1 Results To  BARBARA LYNN SMITH  M D  Resident  Johanna L  Baran  M D  Signed Out by  Melinda J Lerwill  M D             Revised Report            Reason for Addendum  1  Additional Immunohistochemistry Results Reason for Amendment  1  Corrected Transcription Error not in Final Diagnosis CLINICAL DATA  History of left breast invasive lobular carcinoma  status post lumpectomy several years ago  Now with left pleomorphic LCIS on a core  and calcifications on mammogram  Patient is status post radiation and chemotherapy  Stitch marks short  superior and long  lateral  FINAL DIAGNOSIS  BREAST  LEFT   EXCISION  1  INVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA  2  DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU WITH CALCIFICATIONS  3  CHANGES CONSISTENT WITH PRIOR IRRADIATION  4  HEALING BIOPSY SITE  5  SEE NOTE AND TABLE  1  NOTE  The in situ carcinoma demonstrates cellular dyshesion  which raises the possibility of lobular carcinoma in situ  However  some of the carcinoma cells have columnar or polygonal shapes  and focally there is clear evidence of polarization  Immunostains for E cadherin demonstrate patchy to diffuse membranous reactivity in both the lower grade and pleomorphic carcinoma cells  Altogether  the morphologic and immunohistochemical findings support a diagnosis of ductal carcinoma in situ rather than lobular carcinoma in situ  TABLE OF PATHOLOGICAL FINDINGS  1 INVASIVE CARCINOMA TUMOR SIZE  Four small foci ranging from  0 1cm to 0 1cm  GRADE  2  LYMPHATIC VESSEL INVASION  Not identified  BLOOD VESSEL INVASION  Not identified  MARGIN OF INVASIVE CARCINOMA  Invasive carcinoma extends to  0 1cm from the inferior margin of the specimen in one focus  LOCATION OF DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  Within and beyond the region of the invasive carcinoma  GRADE OF DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  2 and 3  MARGIN OF DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  Ductal carcinoma in situ extends to  0 1cm from all the margins  STAINS FOR RECEPTORS  Requested  although there may be insufficient invasive tumor remaining for analysis  By his her signature below  the pathologist listed as making the Final Diagnosis certifies that he she has personally reviewed this case and confirmed or corrected the diagnoses  GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME   REMOVE_MRN and  left breast biopsy   is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 5cm oriented breast tissue with two needle localization wires in place  a short stitch designating  superior   and long stitch designating  lateral   The long axis is anterior to posterior  and the specimen is inked as follows  Superior   blue  inferior   black  medial   green  lateral   yellow  The specimen is serially sectioned to reveal a 0 6cm gritty area  as well as hemorrhagic fibroadipose breast tissue  The gritty area is 0 2cm from the closest superior margin  0 4cm from the closest medial margin  0 7cm from the nearest inferior margin  and 1 2cm from the nearest lateral margin  It is 0 5cm from the anterior surface and 1 5cm from the posterior surface  The specimen is submitted entirely from anterior to posterior in  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   with the gritty area in cassettes  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   ADDENDUM NOTE  REASON  1  QUANTITATIVE RESULTS OF IMMUNOHISTOCHEMICAL STAINING FOR RECEPTOR PROTEINS Breast  Left  block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    The carcinoma in situ tumor cells stain for estrogen receptor protein  The carcinoma in situ tumor cells stain for progesterone receptor protein  The carcinoma in situ tumor cells do not show overexpression of HER 2 c erbB 2  See note  Note  No invasive tumor cells are seen on the tissue sections stained for hormone receptors and HER 2  Estrogen and progesterone receptor quantitation  The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the t